    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm looking into a reclassification request for a parcel in Austin from R-1 to mixed-use. We're hitting a snag with the municipal code where the setback requirements for the new proposed structure seem to conflict with existing encroachment clauses from the neighboring residential lots. Basically, the current encroachments are so close to the property line that meeting the new setback standards might be impossible without a variance. How should I best approach the city planning commission to reconcile these conflicting provisions?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You should approach the City Planning Commission with a detailed comparative analysis, clearly mapping out the existing encroachments against the proposed mixed-use setbacks. It's best to frame your request not just as a conflict, but as a necessary adjustment to allow for responsible, community-benefiting development. You need to demonstrate how the proposed structure, even with the existing encroachments, still meets the spirit of the code while requiring a specific, justifiable variance. A thorough presentation, perhaps with site plans showing the conflict, is your best bet.",
